		Here are Top Interview Questions on Covariant Return Types in Java with answers (both simple and technical):
	
	Q1. What is a covariant return type in Java?
	
	✅ Simple Answer:
	It means an overridden method in a child class can return a subclass of the return type declared in the parent class.
	
	✅ Technical Answer:
	Introduced in Java 5, covariant return types allow method overriding with a more specific return type.
	This improves type safety and avoids explicit casting. It only works with non-primitive return types
	and is valid only in method overriding, not in overloading.
	
	Q2. Why was covariant return type introduced in Java?
	
	✅ Simple Answer:
	To avoid unnecessary type casting when working with overridden methods.

	✅ Technical Answer:
	Before Java 5, the return type had to match exactly. So, if you had:
	
	Animal a = dog.getAnimal();
	Dog d = (Dog) dog.getAnimal(); // had to typecast
	
	
	With covariant return type, the overridden method can directly return Dog, so typecasting is not needed.
	
	Q3. Can covariant return types be used with primitive data types?
	
	✅ Simple Answer:
	No.
	
	✅ Technical Answer:
	Covariant return types only work with object return types. For example, int cannot be replaced with float in overriding. You must use class types like Animal and Dog.
	
	Q4. Can constructors have covariant return types?
	
	✅ Simple Answer:
	No, constructors never have a return type.
	
	✅ Technical Answer:
	Covariant return type is only applicable for method overriding. Constructors don’t participate in inheritance or overriding, so it doesn’t apply there.
	
	Q5. What happens if you try to use an unrelated class as a return type in overriding?
	
	✅ Simple Answer:
	It gives a compilation error.
	
	✅ Technical Answer:
	The return type in the child method must be the same or a subclass of the parent’s return type.
	If you return an unrelated class, Java will throw a compile-time error saying “return type is incompatible”.
	
	Q6. Example Interview Task
	
	👉 Write a program showing covariant return type with Shape and Circle.
	
	✅ Answer:
	
	class Shape {
	    Shape getShape() {
	        return this;
	    }
	}
	
	class Circle extends Shape {
	    @Override
	    Circle getShape() {  // Covariant return type
	        return this;
	    }
	}
	
	public class CovariantExample {
	    public static void main(String[] args) {
	        Shape s = new Circle();
	        s.getShape();  // returns Circle object
	    }
	}